nsition: pre-restart boot_id and healthy state, a background reboot acknowledgement, transient unreachability, a changed boot_id on return (node1 b83fd7ec->c386409c, node2 5e3bb0d6->8cd8ce3d, matching the fixed after-executor snapshot), and recovered sshd/account/key/config/cluster-SSH state verified post-restart for both nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-309cc92060bb475f\",\"cmd-3bb39c7ae1cb44c4\",\"cmd-07ab1cb5d6d64f84\",\"cmd-ee31ac6434654c43\",\"cmd-2a7e45cfbc46448a\",\"cmd-0634693a8e534612\",\"cmd-8b6fd033aa334f82\",\"cmd-9c6b999f8f134ad9\",\"cmd-369e49c60e974967\",\"cmd-7c9010bba8024895\",\"cmd-861480fe42854c88\",\"cmd-4bb0ece7d2fc4529\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands trace clearly to the task outcome or the restart protocol. The account-locking correction (shadow field '!' -> '*' on both nodes) was a legitimate, evidence-supported fix for pubkey auth being blocked by the default locked account, not an unnecessary mutation. Failed initial adduser/ssh attempts were non-destructive discovery/retry steps with no lasting negative effect. The only residue is a trivial /tmp/reboot.log artifact left on both nodes from the reboot command's own output redirection - bounded, expected from the restart protocol, and not a security or functional concern.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e8933331a225477a\",\"cmd-830220ffa42742a3\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor satisfied all three material outcomes of the task: opsbot was created on node1 (and node2), opsbot@node1 has working SSH key-based access to run commands as opsbot on node2, and key-only authentication (password rejected) was enforced and verified on both nodes in both directions. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed cleanly for both nodes, with boot-identity changes matching the fixed after-executor snapshot and full post-restart recovery of the account, key, sshd config, and cross-node SSH functionality. No unexplained or unnecessary mutations were found; the single non-obvious mutation (shadow lock-field change) was a necessary, well-evidenced fix.\",\"limitations\":[\"The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare phase disabled), so preparer-vs-executor state separation relies entirely on the before_prepare/after_executor diff, which shows only expected changes (boot identity, ephemeral ports, /tmp/reboot.log).\",\"Password-rejection tests relied on client-side PreferredAuthentications=password/PubkeyAuthentication=no combined with server-side AuthenticationMethods publickey; the server's own offered-methods behavior was not separately captured via -v verbose output, though the auth log and Match block evidence corroborate publickey-only enforcement.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create an opsbot account on node1 (and correspondingly on node2 as the SSH target)","status":"satisfied","summary":"adduser -D created opsbot on both node1 and node2; getent passwd and /etc/shadow entries confirm uid=1001 account exists with home/.ssh (0700) directory on both nodes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0c9e944950654291","cmd-30143f4bd0b3493e","cmd-97068ad362d44cfe","cmd-cc9f09fcf954440d","cmd-a9a190ae0a314109","cmd-83d3c0a2a6f84a4d"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"opsbot has SSH key-based access to run commands as opsbot on node2, initiated from node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ed25519 keypair generated as opsbot on node1 and its public key installed into node2's opsbot authorized_keys. After discovering and correcting a shadow-lock issue blocking pubkey auth, a live SSH command execution (whoami/hostname/id) from opsbot@node1 to opsbot@node2 succeeded, and was re-verified after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ba19da1193744de0","cmd-83fa5f9e6f984969","cmd-a0d0f55ac3da46fd","cmd-0634693a8e534612","cmd-4bb0ece7d2fc4529"]},{"id":"R3","req